     "text": "How do you make abracadabra abracadabra abracadabra smaller without losing a single letter? Stored as plain bytes it takes 35 bytes, or 280 bits. Two things about it are wasteful. The same 11 letters appear three times, and the letter a makes up almost half of the text while c and d appear once per word. A good compressor should exploit both. Guess before you read on: compressed as a gzip file by zlib at its best setting (level 9), how many bytes do these 35 take? 33 bytes. That looks like almost no saving, but 18 of those bytes are the gzip wrapper: a 10-byte header, then a CRC-32 and the original length (8 bytes) at the end. The DEFLATE stream inside is 15 bytes , 43% of the input. By the end of this page you will have taken it apart bit by bit, and seen an encoder that does it in 13."
